# eth-search — Ethereum Research Data Search

`eth-search` (CLI v2) queries the Meilisearch-based Ethereum research index:
forum posts (ethresear.ch, Ethereum Magicians), EIPs (including draft EIPs
in open PRs), consensus/execution spec code and prose, annotated-spec,
All Core Devs meeting notes, API/networking specs, papers, and manual
imports.

`eth-search --help` and `eth-search <command> --help` are the primary
reference — the epilogs document every mode, flag, and doc_id format with
examples. This skill adds routing guidance the `--help` text doesn't cover:
when to reach for `eth-search` vs `rg`, and which source to scope to.

## Route status and fork questions first

- For the status or complete timeline of one known EIP, use the
  `acd-search` skill and its `eip <number>` route. It reads Forkcast's
  structured history, champions, presentations, call decisions, and Discord
  mentions.
- For call prose, decisions, transcripts, chats, or a topic across calls, use
  `eth-search query "<terms>" --source-name forkcast`. This is ranked text
  retrieval, including the indexed Forkcast transcript and chat families.
- For an exact cross-EIP current-stage set, use
  `eth-search query --fork-stage FORK:STAGE --limit 1000`. For example, use
  `Glamsterdam:Scheduled`. The composite field preserves fork-to-stage
  pairing. An omitted query enumerates the facet and reports truncation.
- Use `--eip N` only for documents authored as EIP N. It searches canonical
  EIPs, labeled draft EIPs, and the labeled Forkcast EIP page on the main
  index. Use `--mentions-eip N` for documents and Discord messages that only
  refer to the EIP.

## eth-search vs rg

- **eth-search**: forum/EIP/spec-prose/meeting-note content, anything
  ranked by relevance or fused lex+vec, cross-source questions ("has X been
  discussed anywhere"), symbol lookups across fork copies (`fn`), EIP
  metadata (`eip`).
- **rg** (or `git grep` in a spec/client repo): you already know the exact
  file or repo to search, want every literal occurrence (not top-k ranked),
  or need a live checkout, not indexed prose (e.g. current `git blame`,
  code outside the indexed repos). `eth-search get` prints the local corpus
  path on every hit specifically so you can drop into `rg`/`sed` for that
  file afterward.

## Commands

| Command | Use |
|---|---|
| `eth-search query "<q>"` | Fused lex+vec search (default). Repeat `-q` to batch reformulations. |
| `eth-search get <id>` | Full text of a chunk (by chunk id) or a whole document/thread (by doc_id). `--context N`, `--thread`. |
| `eth-search eip <number>` | Canonical EIP metadata, labeled per-PR draft deltas, labeled Forkcast context, and forum mentions. No search query needed. |
| `eth-search fn <symbol>` | Exact code symbol lookup: definitions (fork copies grouped by content hash) then references. `--fork electra`. |
| `eth-search status` | Doc counts per source, embedding coverage, freshness (`indexed_as_of`), and the query log's zero-hit rate. Run this first if unsure the index is current. |
| `eth-search kg "<sql>"` | One read-only SELECT over the knowledge graph — relations between things, which no search box can express. See below. |

## Query modes (`--mode`, default `fused`)

- **fused** (default): lexical (semanticRatio 0) and vector (semanticRatio
  1) run in parallel, merged client-side with RRF (k=60). Each hit shows
  `lex#N vec#N` plus raw scores. Right default for most questions.
- **lex**: single keyword call, fastest (~5 ms server-side). Use for exact
  identifiers, function names, EIP numbers, error strings — jargon the
  embedding model doesn't understand scores worse under `vec`.
  Only mode that supports `--sort`.
- **vec**: single embedding call, query auto-expanded and prefixed. Use for
  paraphrased or "how does X work" phrasing with no good keyword anchor.
- **both**: raw lex and vec lists side by side, unfused — use to debug why
  fused ranked something low.

## Source scoping

Filters are typed flags only (`--source-name`, `--source-kind`, `--eip`,
`--mentions-eip`, `--fork-stage`, `--repo`, `--author`, `--eip-status`,
`--since`) — raw filter expressions
are rejected. A filter that matches nothing prints the valid values instead
of an empty list, and `eth-search status` / `--source-name` typos get a
"did you mean" suggestion.

| Question type | Flags |
|---|---|
| EIP details, status, content | `--source-kind eip` |
| Specific EIP specification metadata | `eth-search eip <number>` (not `query`) |
| Known EIP status or timeline | `acd-search eip <number>` through the `acd-search` skill |
| Documents authored as one EIP | `--eip <number>` (main index only) |
| Documents/messages that refer to one EIP | `--mentions-eip <number>` (main and Discord indexes) |
| Exact current fork-stage set | `query --fork-stage FORK:STAGE --limit 1000`; query text is optional |
| Draft EIPs in open PRs | `--source-name eips-draft-pr` |
| ethresear.ch / Magicians discussions | `--source-name ethresearch` / `magicians` |
| Consensus spec prose (p2p, fork choice, validator guides) | `--source-kind spec_prose` |
| Vitalik's annotated spec | `--source-name annotated-spec` |
| All Core Devs / breakout meeting notes | `--source-name acd` |
| Forkcast call summaries, decisions, transcripts, and chats | `--source-name forkcast` |
| JSON-RPC / Engine API | `--source-name execution-apis` |
| Beacon / builder API specs | `--source-name beacon-APIs` / `builder-specs` |
| devp2p, discv5, wire protocols | `--source-name devp2p` |
| Code in consensus-specs / execution-specs | `--repo consensus-specs` (with `query`, code is excluded by default — add `--include-code`) |
| Symbol definition/references across forks | `eth-search fn <symbol> [--fork electra]` (not `query`) |
| Manual / one-off imports | `--source-name manual` (manual imports are `source_kind = generic`; other generic corpora exist too, e.g. papers, vault) |
| Personal vault (Obsidian) | `--source-name vault` |
| Telegram group-chat archives | `--source-name telegram` — technical chats. Chunks are `chat_window`s (a burst of messages), so `author` is the most talkative speaker in the window; `authors` lists them all. Hits point at `corpus/telegram/<chat>/<YYYY-MM>.jsonl` |
| Ethlabs <> partner-org Telegram chats | `--source-name telegram-relations` or `--include-relations` — **excluded from default results**; relationship/company context, the most sensitive corpus material, so retrieve it only when the question is actually about a partner org |
| Granola meeting transcripts | `--source-name transcripts`; use `eth-search status` to verify the live count and freshness |
| Eth R&D Discord dumps | `--source-name discord` or `--channel <topic-dir>` (separate lexical-only index; `lex` and `fused` also merge Discord hits by default, `vec` skips them) |
| Academic papers (OpenAlex) | `--source-name papers` |
| Papers outside OpenAlex (preprints, submissions) | `--source-name manual` (collection `papers`) |
| Recent-only | `--since YYYY-MM-DD` (needs `source_date_ts`; some papers/code chunks have none) |

Run `eth-search status` to see the live source list, doc counts, and
`indexed_as_of` freshness — it's the ground truth over this table if they
disagree.

## Provenance

Every hit that has a corpus file prints `path:start_line` — use it to jump
straight into `rg`/the editor. Repo-derived chunks (specs, EIPs, ACD, API
specs) also carry `source_commit` (the git sha the chunk was extracted at)
and `indexed_as_of` (unix ts, when it was last written to the index) in
`--json` / `get --json` output, so you can trust or distrust a result
without shelling out to `git log`. `eth-search status` shows the newest
`indexed_as_of` per source for a quick staleness check.

## Config

`~/.config/eth-search/config.toml`:
```toml
[server]
url = "http://127.0.0.1:7700"  # env override: ETH_SEARCH_URL
key_file = "~/.config/eth-search/search-key"

[status]
key_file = "~/.config/eth-search/status-key"  # env: ETH_SEARCH_STATUS_KEY_FILE
```
On the Mac, `127.0.0.1:7700` is the restricted SSH forward to NUC
Meilisearch (LaunchAgent `com.erd.meilisearch-tunnel`). If the NUC is not
reachable, the command fails; there is no fallback index.
The optional status key needs `stats.get`; the command continues to use the
ordinary search key for facets and freshness. The status key is read only for
`eth-search status`.
Every invocation appends a private schema-2 JSONL record to
`~/.local/state/eth-search/queries.jsonl`. It includes demand, filters,
duration, outcome, index context, up to eight metadata-only exposed hits, and
linked `get` reads. Result and session identifiers are host-local HMAC values.
The log does not store returned titles, snippets, text, paths, URLs, authors,
or rendered output. The sibling `telemetry.key` is mode `0600`; the directory
and log are mode `0700` and `0600`.

`origin` is one of `organic`, `benchmark`, `replay`, `smoke`, or `review`; it
defaults to `organic` and can be set with `ETH_SEARCH_ORIGIN`. Codex and Claude
session IDs are linked automatically when their host environment provides
them. Set `ETH_SEARCH_REQUEST_ROLE` to `direct_user`, `agent_subquery`, or
`diagnostic` when the default cannot classify the request. `eth-search status`
reports only organic zero-hit rates over the last 7 and 30 days, so evaluation
traffic cannot look like user demand. See `docs/search-telemetry.md` for the
privacy and audit contract.

## Graph queries (`eth-search kg`)

Use `kg` for questions about **relations between things** rather than the
text of things: what an EIP transitively requires, which code implements
or tests a spec section, which threads reference what, how much of each
kind of content exists. `query` finds prose; `kg` answers "what connects
to what" in one statement instead of many searches.

The graph (`data/graph.db`) is NUC-local. The nightly `erd-sync` rebuilds it,
and it is not copied to the Mac. Run `kg` on the NUC:

```bash
ssh nuc 'cd ~/ethereum-research-data && .venv/bin/eth-search kg --schema'
```

On the Mac, plain `eth-search kg` fails with "no knowledge graph"; use the SSH
form above.

```bash
eth-search kg --schema      # live CREATE statements + 3 worked examples
eth-search kg "SELECT to_eip, relation FROM v_eip_dependency WHERE from_eip = 4844"
```

Tables: `node` (forum topics/posts, EIPs and EIP sections, doc sections,
code functions/classes), `v_eip_dependency` (authoritative requires /
supersedes / replaces, canonical first with labeled per-PR draft deltas),
`eip_dependency_edge` (raw dependency facts with source provenance),
`spec_code_link` (EIP → implementing or testing code),
`cross_reference_edge` (mentions_eip / mentions_section / discusses /
references), `code_dependency_edge` (calls / uses_type / imports /
implements_trait). Run `--schema` rather than guessing columns.

Scope: one statement, starting with `SELECT` or `WITH` (writes, `PRAGMA`
and `ATTACH` are refused and the connection is read-only anyway), at most
`--limit` rows (default 100, max 1000), at most 1,000,000 UTF-8 output bytes,
and interrupted after 5 seconds. A byte-limit notice tells you to select fewer
columns or shorter values.

`node.chunk_id` holds the same chunk ids `eth-search get` takes, so a
graph answer converts straight back into text:

```bash
eth-search kg "SELECT n.chunk_id, n.title FROM node n
               JOIN spec_code_link l ON l.code_node_id = n.node_id
               WHERE l.eip = 4844" --limit 20
eth-search get <chunk_id>
```

Two gaps worth knowing: only about 380 EIPs have a `node_type = 'eip'` row (EIP
titles are more reliably found on `eip_section` rows), and there are no
`defines`/`calls` edges for most symbols — `eth-search fn` uses a
chunk-kind heuristic instead.

## Workflow

1. Route: use `acd-search eip <n>` for one EIP's status timeline,
   `eth-search eip <n>` for canonical specification metadata and labeled
   draft deltas, `query --fork-stage` for cross-EIP enumeration, and
   `fn <symbol>` for symbol lookup. Otherwise use `query --mode lex` for exact
   terms and the default `fused` mode for other text questions.
2. Scope with typed filters (table above) if the question names a source.
3. Read `text` in the results — the 2-line snippet is centred on the first
   query term found in the chunk (`…` marks what was cut), so it shows why
   the hit matched; a snippet starting at the chunk head means no query
   term occurs in it, i.e. the vector arm found it. Follow `path:start_line`
   for full context, or `eth-search get <id> --context N` / `--thread`.
4. If the first search misses: try the other mode, drop a filter, or check
   `eth-search status` — the source you want may not be indexed yet.
5. Cite `url` or `path` (with `source_commit`/`indexed_as_of` if precision
   matters) in the answer.

