    12	The hash of a line depends only on the set of declarations that its root
    13	reaches (the closure), not on the traversal order or on the other roots. Thus
    14	a new declaration does not change the line of an old declaration that does
    15	not reach it.
    16	
    17	The lock is a tripwire, not a commitment. `Expr` hashes have 32 bits per node
    18	and ignore binder names, binder info and metadata, so two different
    19	declarations can have one hash. A kernel check, not the lock, makes a proof
    20	valid.
    21	
    22	A declaration is locked if its module is locked and Lean did not generate it,
    23	or if its type (after `whnfR`, so that an `abbrev` alias counts) is a claim, a
    24	profile, a profile entry, a claim domain or a domain map and its module is in
