    85	**Paper proof: canonical roots are injective.** Let `B` and `C` be canonical and suppose `B.root = C.root`. Induct on `B`, with `C` arbitrary.
    86	
    87	If one block is genesis, its root is zero. A canonical node has root `Nat.pair … … + 1`, which is positive. Thus both blocks must be genesis in this case. If both are nodes, write them as `(P,s,r)` and `(Q,t,u)`. Root equality and canonicality give equality of the two successor pairing values. Cancel the successors and apply `Nat.pair_eq_pair`. This gives `P.root = Q.root` and `s = t`. Both parents are canonical. The induction hypothesis gives `P = Q`. The supplied root fields are equal by the original hypothesis, so the two nodes are equal. No slot order, honest majority, signature assumption, synchrony, or common reader is used.
