    89	Canonicality is closed under ancestry by structural induction. It follows that any collection of canonical blocks, including blocks at different readers and times, has injective roots on all its ancestors. Merely checking that a new root is absent from one receiver's store would not give this result: two receivers could accept different blocks under the same root.
    90	
    91	**Paper proof: admission preserves canonical stores.** Start from the actual protocol initial state, whose only stored block is genesis. Induct over the event list. A tick does not add blocks. A HEAD or PC delivery does not add blocks. A rejected proposal leaves the store unchanged. An accepted proposal adds one block whose exact parent is already stored, and whose `rootOK` check succeeded. The parent is canonical by induction, so the new block is canonical. Carried HEAD processing does not add blocks. Existing blocks remain canonical.
