/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:81:**Paper proof: the nonce preserves same-slot siblings.** Fix a closed parent `p` and any slot `s`. For every natural `n`, `mkBlock(p,s,n)` is closed. If two such blocks have equal roots, decode the roots twice to obtain equal nonces. Thus `n ↦ mkBlock(p,s,n)` is injective. For every finite `k`, nonces `0,…,k-1` give `k` distinct siblings. Distinct siblings are incomparable under ancestry: both have depth `p.depth + 1`, and neither equals the other.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:83:This statement concerns expressible blocks. A run still has finitely many events, and admission still requires a suitable parent, slot, time, and proposer signature. The current signature model permits a faulty key holder to sign any number of objects; see [Conditions.lean](scratch/r196/source/AvailableChainStatements/Generic/Conditions.lean), `UnforgeableSignatures`. It does not impose a nonce limit. In contrast, honest `ProtocolSpec.propose` is a deterministic function of state and slot ([Run.lean](scratch/r196/source/AvailableChainModel/Generic/Run.lean), line 73). Use nonce zero, or another specified function of those arguments. An independent honest random choice or external input needs an engine change.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:85:**The r194 scope issues are unchanged.** The new arithmetic changes block identity choices. It does not change which objects the contract names.
