/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:117:A recursive wire check of `Closed` is another valid option. A local wire check is sufficient when `ExecutionValid.deps` supplies an exact parent in a proved closed store. A recursive check removes that dependency from the proof of delivered-tip closure, but costs a walk through the full ancestry. It still excludes malformed deliveries from certified runs.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:119:**Paper proof of the required invariant pattern.** Begin with the store `{genesis}`. Suppose each insertion requires an exact stored parent and a successful local check, and other transitions preserve the block store. Induction on transitions proves that every stored block is closed: old blocks stay closed, and each new node has a closed parent and a valid local root. This result holds for arbitrary event lists from init, without wire validity. It does not cover arbitrary hand-built states with malformed initial blocks.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:123:**CONJECTURE — concrete integration.** After the listed changes and the remaining anchor-invariant proofs, all four protocols can discharge `RootInjective` from `ExecutionValid`. This review does not supply a Lean proof of the combined invariant. Grid CO's obligation anchors, L*'s lock, and SA-PC's selected PC targets must all be closed when used, including after `clock`. Existing path and store facts support this work; they are not themselves a proof of the new root invariant. In particular, [SAPC Paths.lean](scratch/r196/source/AvailableChainSAPC/Proofs/Paths.lean), lines 12–79, proves path closure and that a HEAD or PC envelope does not insert a block.
