/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:150:An AP-to-FG projection must preserve root and ancestry and be deterministic for a canonical AP identity. It can decode rows from the tail or validate separately supplied rows against it. If the tail also encodes input or other body data, validate that full code and retain its root in the FG view. Do not demand that the same root also equal a different, smaller codec of only FG-visible fields. Keep r194's checked-wrapper versus pinned-`on_block` decision, store proof, cross-reader proof, and codec/domain conventions. **CONJECTURE — composition implementation:** such a tail codec and projection can avoid a shared `Block` constructor change in the eventual FG composition. No decoder, wrapper, or composition theorem was implemented here.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:152:**I1 remains open.** The existing proposer signature has no run-chosen input argument. A zero nonce still lets every node compute the proposal from public state and slot. A free nonce supplies possible identities; it does not supply the required input or bind a proposal to it. [NoInput.lean](scratch/r196/source/AvailableChainRegistry/Gaps/NoInput.lean), lines 7–25, explicitly requires both a run-chosen proposer input and a floor claim that binds the proposal to that input. The lifted clock-only protocol must be refuted against that claim. Keep the existing I1 witness and its `KNOWN-GAP` status.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:154:**CONJECTURE — future representation choice:** engine v2 could pass an input to the proposer, encode it injectively in the nonce tail, and define the input-binding claim through a decoder. This could meet the representation need without a new shared `Block` field. It still needs the engine API, input semantics, decoder laws, and the new claim and refutation. An explicit body field may be easier to specify and use. r194's 1–3 week shared-type migration is therefore not a necessary cost of preserving sibling identities, and is not proved necessary for I1 either. The bare free-nonce rule is not an I1 solution.
