/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:170:[Accumulation.lean](scratch/r196/source/AvailableChainWitnesses/Accumulation.lean), lines 10–30, currently uses roots `1,2,3`. Its parent is a slot-zero node. The proposed root rule permits this node; slot increase is a separate rule. Its pooled head uses `Vote` tags and counts, not root arithmetic: six parent votes, two visible votes, then three hidden votes. The replacement children have the same parent and slot and remain incomparable. Thus the fork and the count-based switch remain expressible. This is a separate toy protocol with `interface.block := none`, not a run of a repaired base protocol. The minimal fix requires no edit to it.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:172:[CleanInvariantCounterexample.lean](scratch/r196/source/AvailableChainSAPC/Tests/CleanInvariantCounterexample.lean), lines 7–28, is more directly affected. It uses two slot-1 children of genesis and requires `x.root > y.root`. Nonces `1` and `0` give that order and two valid distinct blocks. Its slot-1 proposer is faulty, so using two nonces does not contradict honest nonce zero. Its existing two-branch scenario is therefore still expressible without a new body field. **CONJECTURE — complete Lean regression:** recoding these fixtures and updating the guard proofs preserves the full compiled witness. Python checked the encodings, fork relations, and required order, not the complete SA-PC execution or all its proof obligations.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:176:The new encoding need not preserve old numeric comparisons between blocks with different parents or slots. It also forces each valid node root to exceed its parent root. Do not claim equivalence of all old and new traces. Check concrete tie cases in Grid CO, SA-PC, L*, and any FG composition.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:196:Decoding adds two integer square roots per local check. The local [Batteries implementation](scratch/r196/source/.lake/packages/batteries/Batteries/Data/Nat/Basic.lean), lines 92–108, uses Newton iteration with division. It is not a linear scan through all naturals. Even so, larger integers increase storage and arithmetic work. A large free nonce can increase the root at one shallow step: for fixed parent and slot, sufficiently large `n` gives inner size on the order of `n²` and root size on the order of `n⁴`. There is no input-size bound in this proposal. Recursive validation repeats work along ancestry; exact structural equality and membership tests can also revisit large blocks.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-nonce.md:200:Use symbolic lemmas for `rootOK (mkBlock p s n)`, ancestry closure, and injectivity. Prove them with the inverse laws rather than reducing large numerals. Keep computation tests shallow and use known-parent closure at admission. A future efficient injective bit-string codec can reduce this particular growth, but needs its own proof. Truncating roots or taking them modulo a fixed width loses the exact injectivity theorem. An unbounded exact code is not a fixed-width cryptographic hash and does not prove data availability or signature security.
