/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:50:The `+ 1` is required: it keeps every node root separate from genesis, including a slot-zero node. Increasing slots remain a separate protocol condition. Mathlib's local [Data/Nat/Pairing.lean](scratch/r194/Mathlib-Nat-Pairing.lean):38 defines the pairing function, and `:81` proves `Nat.pair_eq_pair`. It is not necessary to introduce an axiom or to guess a library theorem name. The function is `b*b+a` when `a<b`, and `a*a+a+b` otherwise. With this codec, slot 1 on genesis has root 2, slot 2 on that block has root 9, and slot 4 on genesis has root 17.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:52:`rootOK` checks one node. `Canonical` checks its full ancestry. The known-parent admission rule supplies the induction from the first to the second. A recursive Boolean checker can also be provided, with a theorem that it is equivalent to `Canonical`; it is useful for imported paths and standalone objects. It need not be rerun over the full path at every admission into an already proved canonical store.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:54:This encoding commits to all fields of the **current structural block**, apart from the root field which stores the encoding. It does not commit to proposal views, carried signatures, support metadata, or a PC: those are fields of the message, not of `Block`. Two messages can therefore carry the same block and different metadata. Do not describe this design as a hash of the complete proposal wire object.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:56:A full-content design must first specify what is part of block identity. If transaction input, rows, or other data become block fields, encode those fields too. An opaque digest of that content simply moves the collision assumption to the digest unless its model has an injective codec. Including the supplied root itself in the input to its own codec is not the proposed construction.
