/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:75:For Grid CO, extend the refusal condition in `admitProposal` (`Protocol.lean:313–325`) with `!rootOK b`. Keep its known-parent, increasing-slot, proposer, freshness, and future-slot checks. Extend the proposal arm of `wellFormed` (`:333–342`) with `rootOK b`. `depsPresent` already requires the exact structural parent in the store. A local root check there would be redundant. Prefer a named validity projection lemma to more nested `.1.1` accesses in proofs.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:77:SA uses `GridCO.process`, `GridCO.propose`, and `GridCO.gridCO` through aliases in `SA/Model/Protocol.lean:83–99`. The shared proof must quantify over `LeasePolicy`; one proof then covers both policies. `Registry.GridCO.gridCOx` changes only `excludes`, so its stores and validity check inherit the fix too. Its distinct protocol type still needs a wrapper theorem or a run conversion.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:79:For SA-PC, `Handlers.proposalBase` already calls `GridCO.admitProposal` with an empty carried-HEAD list. `proposalEnters` tests the resulting store change, and the full handler handles raw HEADs and PC members only after that test. The shared admission guard therefore applies at the right boundary. However, `SAPC.wellFormed` is an independent definition (`Handlers.lean:61–72`); it needs its own proposal root check. Updating Grid CO alone does not update it. Neither a HEAD nor a direct PC envelope introduces a base block (`SAPC/Proofs/Paths.lean:55`), and `pcPaths` requires admitted paths. They need no new block insertion rule for the stated result.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:81:For L*, update its separate proposer, proposal handler, and wire validator. Its handler currently checks the future slot, duplicate block, and known parent. Unlike Grid CO, it leaves proposer-slot and increasing-slot checks to `wellFormed`. The root proof can work without changing that split: make the new root guard explicit in the handler, and retain the existing wire checks. A broader admission cleanup is a separate decision.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:83:For the present `RootInjective` scope, HEAD validation need not change. Grid CO and L* can retain a malformed, unresolved HEAD target; SA-PC can archive one. These facts must not be confused with admitting its block. Resolution and PC validity require the corresponding block or path in the block store. Rejecting all malformed HEAD fields would be a wider message-validity change: it can affect vote counts, equivocation detection, archive behavior, and relay proofs. Do not add it silently as part of this fix.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:87:If one block is genesis, its root is zero. A canonical node has root `Nat.pair … … + 1`, which is positive. Thus both blocks must be genesis in this case. If both are nodes, write them as `(P,s,r)` and `(Q,t,u)`. Root equality and canonicality give equality of the two successor pairing values. Cancel the successors and apply `Nat.pair_eq_pair`. This gives `P.root = Q.root` and `s = t`. Both parents are canonical. The induction hypothesis gives `P = Q`. The supplied root fields are equal by the original hypothesis, so the two nodes are equal. No slot order, honest majority, signature assumption, synchrony, or common reader is used.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:89:Canonicality is closed under ancestry by structural induction. It follows that any collection of canonical blocks, including blocks at different readers and times, has injective roots on all its ancestors. Merely checking that a new root is absent from one receiver's store would not give this result: two receivers could accept different blocks under the same root.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:91:**Paper proof: admission preserves canonical stores.** Start from the actual protocol initial state, whose only stored block is genesis. Induct over the event list. A tick does not add blocks. A HEAD or PC delivery does not add blocks. A rejected proposal leaves the store unchanged. An accepted proposal adds one block whose exact parent is already stored, and whose `rootOK` check succeeded. The parent is canonical by induction, so the new block is canonical. Carried HEAD processing does not add blocks. Existing blocks remain canonical.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:93:This proof works for arbitrary event lists of the fixed handlers, including malformed deliveries, without `ExecutionValid`. It is a property of reachable stores; it is not a theorem about arbitrary hand-built `State` values. For SA-PC it is the base block store that is canonical. Extend this invariant to all proposal anchors: Grid CO obligations come from stored candidates; L* locks come from stored candidates; SA-PC PC targets have admitted paths. Genesis fallback and a GHOST step then preserve canonicality. In particular, honest code never builds on a noncanonical anchor. Existing path, target, and PC-validity lemmas give much of this argument, but this new combined invariant still needs a Lean proof.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:95:Consequently, no two distinct blocks admitted by these handlers, at any modeled readers or times from init, can have the same root. A faulty proposer can create valid competing blocks, with different canonical roots. It cannot create a collision among those admitted blocks. This conclusion is stronger than a collision claim restricted to one local store.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:105:Consider a non-genesis `BlockInRun` witness. If it comes from a delivery, `hx.wire` gives the new local root check and `hx.deps` gives membership of the parent in the receiving store before that delivery. The store invariant makes the parent canonical. Hence the delivered tip is canonical, even if the handler rejects it for some other reason. If it comes from emission, the proposal action uses `mkBlock` on a canonical anchor. The clock before emission and all possible proposal anchors must be covered by the state invariant. The interface returns no tip from other message forms. Ancestry closure makes the named block canonical. Apply canonical root injectivity to the two named blocks.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:109:The phrase “all runs” needs qualification. The existing raw `Run` permits any event list, including a rejected malformed proposal. Its old `BlockInRun` therefore prevents an unconditional theorem `∀ rho, RootInjective E I rho`, even with the fixed handlers. The valid statement is **all `ExecutionValid` runs**, plus an unconditional canonical-store theorem for all raw event lists. An all-raw-run theorem for the old predicate would require a type restriction, a different `Interface.block`, or a different named scope. Those are different designs.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:111:This still uses the engine's existing admission idealization. `ExecutionValid.wire` excludes malformed delivery events from the certified run. The engine does not execute `wellFormed` as a dispatch guard (`World.step`, `Run.lean:103–105`). The explicit handler guard provides operational rejection in the modeled code, but the run-level theorem does not certify a trace that records rejected malformed deliveries. Such a trace needs an accepted-block scope or an explicit network-input layer. Also, schedules are honest-only: this does not constrain a Byzantine node's private memory. “Faulty blocks are never admitted by anyone” is too broad. The supported statement is that blocks with invalid encodings never enter reachable stores through the fixed handlers. Valid blocks from faulty proposers remain allowed.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:116:FGBlock.node parent slot root (rows : List (Row V))
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:119:Two such nodes can have the same parent and slot but different rows. Pairing only `(parent.root, slot)` assigns the same root to both, and the canonical-block proof fails at the row field. This is not fixed by admitting one of them at each reader.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:137:These encodings are injective by tag separation, pairing injectivity, signer-code injectivity, and list induction. Keep the list in its exact order, including duplicates. `FGBlock` equality distinguishes list order; the chain transition folds rows in order. Encoding a set of rows, sorting the list, or omitting a field would not prove equality of the current type. The same canonical-parent induction now proves injectivity for canonical `FGBlock`s, including row equality.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:139:Add a local `fgRootOK` refusal to `Store.on_block` before `update_finality`, with the existing parent-membership condition. Alternatively, leave the generic pinned function unchanged and add a checked AP admission wrapper which calls it only for a correctly encoded block. The wrapper must be the only path into the composed reachable store. Prove canonicality from `Store.init`, then derive `ViewRootInj` for a view of its blocks and `RootInjectiveOn B W` for any two canonical chain tips. For two readers, prove both stores use the same codec; one local `ViewRootInj` does not suffice for cross-reader comparison.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:141:`StoreInv` alone does not imply canonical roots. Keep a separate canonical-store invariant, or extend a new reachable-store wrapper with it. Pure KitFG theorems quantify over arbitrary blocks and views. Their explicit `ViewRootInj` and `RootInjectiveOn` hypotheses remain useful. Reachable-state corollaries can discharge these hypotheses. Do not simply delete them from the generic theorem statements.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:143:The AP-to-FG map also needs an identity law. Mapping two AP messages with one structural `Block` and different row lists to different FG blocks under the same AP root is unsound. The composed block identity must include the ordered rows, and the map must preserve roots and ancestry. If it projects away AP data, it must not assign different FG content to the same canonical AP root. One full-body codec and a proved projection are preferable to independently recomputing unrelated AP and FG roots. Cross-type or cross-version root comparison also needs a declared domain convention; use explicit tags if these namespaces share a root space. Shared genesis zero must be intentional.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:145:The concrete `fgRoot` above is a standalone codec for exactly the fields of `FGBlock`. If a composed AP block also commits to data that the FG projection removes, its root will generally differ from that standalone codec. In that case, validate the complete AP body once, preserve its root in the FG projection, and derive FG root injectivity from AP injectivity and the deterministic projection. Do not also require the projected root to equal the smaller standalone `fgRoot`. Alternatively, extend the FG view with the content needed by the common codec. This choice belongs in the composition interface.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:147:**What remains an idealization.** `Nat.pair` is an unbounded exact encoding, not a fixed-width cryptographic hash. No injective map exists from all unbounded block content into a fixed finite hash space. A later finite-hash implementation needs a collision-resistance assumption, a probabilistic statement, or a refinement restricted to collision-free traces. Root checking does not prove signature security, data availability, payload validity, proposer eligibility, or implementation conformance.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:153:**Impact on block identity and existing counterexamples.** Under the narrow codec, same-parent, same-slot nodes are one block. Today the root field can distinguish them. This change can remove an adversarial choice, even where the removed examples had no root collision. It is appropriate only if the structural model intentionally treats those nodes as the same content.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:157:If same-slot distinct content must remain possible, add an explicit content field, for example a `Nat` code for a modeled body, and encode `(parent.root, slot, content)`. For FG the rows already provide such content. For the base `Block`, adding a field changes the shared inductive type and every constructor case. This has much broader impact than a helper codec. Coordinate that decision with I1 and engine v2; an arbitrary nonce can preserve abstract distinct bodies, but it does not by itself give the proposer the run-chosen input required by I1.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:159:**Proof changes for the narrow first implementation.** Add helper facts for nonzero node roots, canonical construction, ancestry closure, and root injectivity. Add store-preservation, proposal-anchor, emission, delivery, and run-scope lemmas. A shared Grid CO proof covers SA. SA-PC needs a base-store lift and its PC anchor argument. L* needs its own reachable lock and store argument.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:163:Handler and wire proof repairs are the larger task. The source scan found references to `admitProposal`, `wellFormed`, `proposalBase`, or `proposalEnters` in 15 Grid CO files, 31 SA-PC files, one L* file, and three Registry files. These counts include model and test files; they are an audit set, not a measured list of compilation failures. Many state-preservation proofs split on the refusal branch and should need only small repairs. Acceptance theorems need a positive root-validity fact. The full file list is in the inventory below.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:167:- Grid CO `Paths.handled_proposal_stored`, `accepted_proposal_handled_stored`, and `ProposalDelivery.delivered_proposal_stored`: acceptance now requires the new check. Obtain it from wire validity or a previous successful admission. For lemmas over arbitrary states and blocks, strengthen the local hypotheses explicitly.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:168:- Grid CO `MergedViews.stored_proposal_wellFormed`, `wellFormed_support_subset`, `Receipts`, `Stamps`, `ReceiptOrigins`, `RecordSlots`, and `Map`: preserve shape projections and the extra refusal branch.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:169:- SA-PC `BaseViews.proposalEnters_data`, `ProposalDelivery.process_proposal_stored` and `delivered_proposal_accepted`, `ReceiptRelay.accepted_handled_processed`, and the base admission lemmas: carry the check through the projection. Several proofs use fixed nested conjunction projections from `wellFormed`; these will be fragile after a new conjunct.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:170:- `Registry/GridCOHooks.lean`: recheck handler unfolding in monotonicity and permanent-refusal proofs. Root checks are state independent, but their addition changes branch syntax. No new `excludes` rule is needed for the recommended design.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:183:For the generic regimes, deleting the field changes the contract for **every** `ProtocolSpec` and arbitrary `Interface`. The new protocol proofs do not justify that. `Framework.Instance` has only the protocol, time unit, interface, and `HooksSound`; it has no root-soundness law. An arbitrary interface can name colliding blocks independently of its handler.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:185:If removal is required, make the replacement explicit. One option is a proved instance law with the shape `∀ E rho, ExecutionValid P E rho → RootInjective E iface rho`, and regimes without the redundant field for instances carrying that law. Another is a separate facet and catalog domain for such instances. A third is a new accepted-block scope, with a complete audit of proposal-origin and emitted-proposal uses. Each option changes more than protocol code. Do not make a global instance for a class which is false for arbitrary protocols, or present a typeclass assumption as a derived theorem.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:189:**Catalog lock, registry certificates, and FG pins.** The lock hashes the transitive declaration closure, not source-file text. `scripts/CatalogLock.lean:55–70` selects framework modules and catalog/facet declarations. `ownHash` and `deps` at `:118–134` exclude theorem proof bodies from hashes, but include definition bodies and theorem types. The separate axiom scan still checks proofs.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:191:For the recommended variant, adding a codec module and changing concrete protocol definitions need not change any existing lock line. The generic catalog is parameterized by `Instance`; it does not enumerate Grid CO or SA-PC protocol bodies. Keep `Block` and `Block.root` unchanged, keep the generic regime definitions unchanged, and place new concrete wrappers outside the locked catalog. Adding unrelated declarations to an imported module does not itself change the closure of an old declaration. This is an expected result from source inspection, not a lock check performed here. Rebuild and run the lock check before asserting it.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:193:Changing the body of `Block.root`, changing the `Block` constructors, narrowing `BlockInRun`, removing generic regime fields, or adding a required root law to `Framework.Instance` changes declarations reached by existing catalog entries. Expect changed lock lines and a deliberate catalog migration, even if some claim names remain the same. The newly generated 199-entry lock is the baseline for that comparison. A recent regeneration is not a reason to accept another changed lock without reviewing its semantic cause.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:199:FG's `RootInjectiveOn` supports `uniqueJust_chains`, `finalLock`, `accountable`, and `CrossingNoFinal`; `ViewRootInj` supports selection, frame correspondence, `InBandLock`, and `TwoChanges`. Keep those pure statements and discharge their hypotheses in new canonical-run corollaries. This leaves their existing pin meanings intact. A new wrapper can reuse the old `on_block` theorem. If the pinned `on_block` itself gains a refusal, repair `on_block_of_refused`, `on_block_of_accepted`, `storeInv_on_block`, and the store examples, and review the `fj.frame.v3` correspondence and pin prose. Do not silently remove root hypotheses from `Pins.lean` or claim the archived source already specified the new check. FG pins and `catalog.lock` are separate checks.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:201:**Tests, witnesses, and expected size.** Keep raw constructors available for negative tests. Replace positive accepted-block fixtures with the canonical constructor only after checking that they still express the same scenario. `GridCO/Tests/Smoke`, `SA/Tests/Smoke`, and `SAPC/Tests/Smoke` contain arbitrary root literals. Some tests use hand-built states: their raw values may remain valid inputs to generic lemmas, but such states are not automatically reachable under the fixed handler. The SA-PC clean-invariant counterexample needs the identity review described above.
/Users/francesco/ethereum/research/async-resilient-committees/tip-stability/codex/root-design.md:205:`Witnesses/Sanity` uses `⟨s+1⟩`; `Witnesses/Regimes` names one literal block; `Witnesses/Accumulation` has two same-slot children with distinct root literals. These are separate toy protocols, not runs of Grid CO, SA, SA-PC, or L*. The narrow fix does not require changing them. In particular, recoding Accumulation's children to the same block would destroy its fork witness. A global `Block` restriction would force a wider redesign of these examples.
